# Semantic Versioning (SemVer) & Release Numbering

Derive release version numbers strictly from the semantic content of the commits since the previous release (`vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`), never by treating version components as base-10 digits that roll over at `.9`:

- **PATCH (`x.y.Z`)**: Bug fixes, security patches, refactors, test/CI adjustments, or internal hardening without new user-facing capabilities (`fix:`, `perf:`, `refactor:`, `chore:`, `ci:`). Multi-digit patch numbers (`1.5.10`, `1.5.11`, `1.5.42`) are standard SemVer — increment `Z` (`1.5.9 -> 1.5.10`) when the release contains only patch-level changes.
- **MINOR (`x.Y.0`)**: Backward-compatible new features, new commands, new configuration options, or new public capabilities (`feat:`). Increment `Y` and reset `Z` to `0` (`1.5.9 -> 1.6.0`) when the release introduces new features.
- **MAJOR (`X.0.0`)**: Incompatible breaking changes to public contracts, CLI flags, workflow inputs, or configuration schemas (`BREAKING CHANGE:` or `<type>!:`).

## Examples

```text
Previous tag: v1.5.9
Commits: fix(sandbox): allow /run in landrun for DNS resolution
Next tag: v1.5.10 (NOT v1.6.0)

Previous tag: v1.5.9
Commits: feat(orchestrator): add 3-way Git branch sync and event-driven CI continuation
Next tag: v1.6.0
```
